            <ArticleTitle>Delays in the treatment of cauda equina syndrome due to its variable clinical features in patients presenting to the emergency department.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>33-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify reasons for delay in management of patients with cauda equina syndrome (CES) and to determine commonly presented features of CES.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospectively, the presenting features and management of patients treated for CES over a 4-year period were reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Reasons for delay in treatment were identified in over half of the patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Most patients do not present with all the characteristic features of CES. Sacral sensory loss is a sensitive and relatively specific sign for diagnosing CES.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
